        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Replacing a water heater with tankless is not a swap. It is a small renovation of the corner your old tank sat in: a bigger gas line, a new vent, a drain, an outlet and a set of service valves. None of it is exotic, but every piece is extra work, and together they decide whether the project costs a little more than a new tank or several thousand dollars more.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer: It Is a Conversion, Not a Swap
          </h2>

          <p>
            When a tank water heater fails and you replace it with another tank, the installer disconnects the old one, sets the new one in the same spot, and reconnects the same gas line, the same flue, the same water lines and the same relief drain. Nothing upstream changes, which is why a like-for-like replacement is usually a two-to-four-hour job and why the price is predictable.
          </p>

          <p>
            Replacing a tank with a tankless unit breaks almost every one of those assumptions. A whole-house gas tankless heater burns fuel at four to five times the rate of the tank it replaces, so the gas pipe is often too small. It vents differently, so the old flue usually cannot be reused. It needs electricity even when it burns gas. And because it hangs on a wall rather than sitting on the floor, the water connections move too.
          </p>

          <p>
            None of this means converting is a bad idea. A well-installed tankless heater can run for 20 years, frees up floor space, and never runs out mid-shower as long as it is sized for your flow. If you are still deciding whether tankless suits your house at all, our <a href="/what-is-a-tankless-water-heater/">plain explanation of how a tankless water heater works</a> and the broader <a href="/types-of-water-heaters/">guide to the types of water heaters</a> are the better starting points. This page assumes you are leaning yes and want to know what the job involves.
          </p>

          <p>
            The money follows the work. Our <a href="/tankless-water-heater-cost/">tankless water heater cost breakdown</a> puts a gas tankless replacing a tank at roughly $3,000 to $6,500 installed, against roughly $1,500 to $3,000 to replace a gas tank with another tank. The gap is almost entirely the gas line, the venting and the labor to rebuild them. If your house happens to have a large gas line and an easy outside wall nearby, you land at the bottom of that range. If it does not, you land at the top.
          </p>

