        <div id="gas-line" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. The Gas Line: Why the Old Pipe Usually Is Not Enough
          </h2>

          <p>
            A typical 40- or 50-gallon gas tank is rated somewhere around 30,000 to 50,000 BTU per hour. A whole-house tankless heater is usually rated between 150,000 and 199,000 BTU per hour, because it heats water as it flows instead of slowly warming a stored tankful. That burst of demand is the single biggest reason conversions cost more than people expect.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most tanks are fed by a half-inch gas line, which is plenty for 40,000 BTU. Whether a half-inch line can carry nearly 200,000 BTU depends on its length and on everything else drawing from the same pipe, and in most houses the answer is no once you get past a very short run. The installer sizes the pipe from the code tables using the total connected load and the longest run from the meter, and the common result is a new three-quarter-inch line, sometimes larger, run back toward the meter or to a trunk line that can carry the load.
          </p>

          <p>
            The meter itself is the next check. Many residential gas meters are rated around 250 cubic feet per hour, which is roughly 250,000 BTU per hour of natural gas. Add a 199,000 BTU tankless heater to a furnace, a range, a dryer and perhaps a fireplace, and the total connected load can exceed what the meter is rated to deliver. The fix is a call to the gas utility for a larger meter, which is usually inexpensive or free but can add days or weeks to the schedule.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tankless heaters are also fussier about pressure than tanks are. The installation manual specifies a minimum inlet gas pressure, and if the pressure sags when the furnace and the heater fire together, the heater can throw an error code or deliver lukewarm water. A good installer checks the static and running pressure at the heater with everything on, not just whether the burner lights.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE GAS RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Add up every gas appliance before you buy the heater.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Ask the installer to total the connected BTU load and measure the run from the meter on the first visit. That one calculation tells you whether you need a short new line, a long new line, a bigger meter, or a smaller heater, and it is where most surprise charges come from.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="venting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Venting and Condensate: The Old Flue Gets Retired
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most older gas tanks are atmospheric: hot exhaust rises naturally through a draft hood into a metal B-vent flue or a masonry chimney. A gas tankless heater is a sealed, fan-driven appliance that pushes its exhaust under positive pressure. Manufacturers do not allow that exhaust into a B-vent, so the existing flue is typically abandoned and a new vent is run, usually straight out the nearest exterior wall.
          </p>

          <p>
            What the new vent is made of depends on the heater. Non-condensing units run hot exhaust and usually need Category III stainless steel venting or a manufacturer-approved equivalent, which is expensive per foot. Condensing units extract so much heat that the exhaust leaves cool enough for PVC, CPVC or polypropylene pipe, which is cheap and easy to run. That difference alone can make a more expensive condensing heater the cheaper project overall, and it is covered in detail in our <a href="/condensing-vs-non-condensing-tankless-water-heater/">condensing vs non-condensing comparison</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Condensing heaters create a new chore: condensate. They produce a steady trickle of mildly acidic water that has to go to a floor drain, a standpipe or a condensate pump. Many manufacturers recommend, and some local codes require, a neutralizer cartridge filled with limestone or similar media to raise the pH before the water reaches cast-iron drains. Budget for the drain route and a neutralizer when you plan a condensing unit, and replace the media every year or two as the manual directs.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is one more venting trap that catches people. In many houses the old water heater shared a flue with the furnace. Remove the water heater, and the furnace can be left alone in a flue sized for two appliances, which can draft poorly and condense inside a masonry chimney. Installers call this an orphaned furnace. The fix is usually a properly sized chimney liner or a new vent for the furnace, and it should be checked before the job is priced rather than discovered on the day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where the vent comes out matters too. The manual and local code set minimum clearances from windows, doors, air intakes, gas meters, decks and the ground, and those rules can push the heater to a different wall than you expected. If the nearest clear spot is a long run away, the manual also caps the maximum vent length and number of elbows, which may favor a different location entirely.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="power-water" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Power, Water Quality and Valves
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most atmospheric gas tanks need no electricity at all. A gas tankless heater needs a 120-volt outlet near the unit for its fan, controls and ignition. That is usually a short, cheap circuit, but it has two consequences: an electrician may need to be part of the job, and the heater stops working during a power outage unless you plug it into a backup source. If you lose power often, that is worth knowing before you remove a tank that would have kept working.
          </p>

          <p>
            Electric whole-house tankless heaters are a different scale of problem. An electric tank typically runs on a single 30-amp, 240-volt circuit. A whole-house electric tankless unit is commonly 18 to 36 kW and needs two to four large double-pole breakers, with total draw that can exceed 100 amps on the biggest models. Many houses with 100-amp or even 150-amp service simply cannot add that without a service upgrade. Our <a href="/what-size-tankless-water-heater-do-i-need/">tankless sizing guide</a> shows how to work out the kW you actually need, and in cold-groundwater regions the answer is often more than the panel can give.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water quality matters more on a tankless heater than on a tank. The heat exchanger has narrow passages, and hard water deposits scale in them faster than it settles to the bottom of a tank. Above roughly 7 grains per gallon, many manufacturers recommend treatment or more frequent descaling, and some tie warranty coverage to water quality. If your water is hard, factor in a <a href="/best-water-softener-system/">water softener</a> or at least a scale-reduction cartridge, and commit to annual flushing.
          </p>

          <p>
            That flushing is only practical if the installer fits isolation valves, the pair of service valves with hose ports that let you close off the heater and circulate descaling solution through it. They add little to the parts bill and turn a plumber visit into a 90-minute DIY job. Insist on them in the quote, along with a pressure relief valve if the model does not include one, and a check of whether your local code requires a thermal expansion tank on a closed system.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, think about recirculation. A tankless heater does not deliver hot water any faster to a distant bathroom than a tank does, and some people notice the wait more because of the short burst of cold water between back-to-back uses. Many units support a recirculation pump or a crossover valve under the farthest sink. Deciding this during installation is far easier than adding it later.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="location" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Where the New Heater Goes
          </h2>

          <p>
            The appeal of tankless is partly physical: a unit roughly the size of a carry-on suitcase hangs on the wall and frees the floor space a 50-gallon tank used to take. In practice most conversions mount the new heater on the wall right above or beside the old tank location, because the water lines are already there and the vent route is known. Moving it farther than that adds plumbing, and every extra foot of hot-water pipe adds to the wait at the tap.
          </p>

          <p>
            In warm climates, an outdoor-rated model mounted on an exterior wall avoids indoor venting entirely, since the unit exhausts directly from its own cabinet. That can make the cheapest conversion of all. Outdoor units have freeze protection heaters that need power, and the water lines feeding them need insulation, so they suit places where hard freezes are rare and power outages short.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clearances still apply indoors. The manual lists minimum distances to the ceiling, the floor and the sides for service access and combustion air, and the wall has to carry the weight of a filled unit. A garage or basement wall framed in studs is usually fine; a crumbling block wall may need a mounting board. In a closet, check that the door does not block service access or the drain route for the pressure relief valve.
          </p>

          <p>
            If the household has one bathroom far from the others, or a kitchen at the opposite end of a long house, it is worth asking whether one central heater is the right design at all. A smaller main heater plus a <a href="/point-of-use-water-heater/">point of use water heater</a> at the remote fixture can beat one large unit on both the gas line and the wait for hot water.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="permits-timeline" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Permits, Inspection, Timeline and DIY vs Pro
          </h2>

          <p>
            Almost every jurisdiction in the US requires a permit to replace a water heater, and a conversion involves exactly the work inspectors care most about: new gas piping, a new vent through the building envelope and new electrical. Expect the inspector to check the gas line sizing and a pressure test on new piping, the vent material, length and termination clearances, the relief valve discharge, and the bonding and electrical connections. A licensed installer pulls the permit as part of the job; ask to see it.
          </p>

          <p>
            A straightforward conversion with a short gas run and an easy outside wall is typically done in one long day. Add a long gas line, a roof vent, an orphaned furnace flue or a condensate pump, and it can stretch to two. If the gas utility needs to upgrade the meter, or an electric unit needs a service upgrade, the schedule can stretch to weeks. That is the strongest argument for planning a conversion before the old tank fails rather than the morning it floods the basement.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your tank has already failed and you need hot water tonight, the practical move is often a like-for-like tank replacement now and a planned conversion later, or not at all. Our guide to <a href="/how-long-do-water-heaters-last/">how long water heaters last</a> explains how to read the serial number for the age of your tank, which tells you how much runway you have to plan.
          </p>

          <p>
            Can you do it yourself? Mounting the unit and re-piping the water lines are within reach of a capable DIYer. Gas piping, venting and the electrical circuit are a different matter: many jurisdictions require a licensed professional for gas work, and most manufacturers condition the warranty on a licensed installation. Because the heat exchanger warranty is often the most valuable part of a tankless purchase, a DIY install that voids it is rarely a saving.
          </p>

          <p>
            When you collect quotes, make them itemize the gas line, vent, condensate, electrical, valves and permit separately. A single bottom-line price hides whether one installer skipped the gas line upgrade that another included. Ask which brands they service locally too; a heater with no nearby parts or trained techs is a liability, which is part of why <a href="/water-heater-brands/">water heater brand support</a> matters as much as the spec sheet.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="when-not-to" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. When You Should Not Convert
          </h2>

          <p>
            Tankless is not the right answer for every house, and the conversion cost makes the wrong choice expensive. The clearest case against it is a small household with modest hot water use on a cheap gas rate. The fuel savings of a tankless over a decent tank are often under $100 a year, so a conversion that costs $2,000 to $4,000 more than a new tank may take decades to pay back, if it ever does.
          </p>

          <p>
            The second case is an all-electric home with limited panel capacity. A whole-house electric tankless heater can need a service upgrade that costs as much as the heater itself, and in a cold climate it may still struggle to keep up with two showers in January. For most of those homes a <a href="/best-heat-pump-water-heater/">heat pump water heater</a> is the better buy: it uses a fraction of the electricity of a standard electric tank, runs on an ordinary circuit, and qualifies for larger rebates in many areas.
          </p>

          <p>
            The third case is a house where the gas line, meter and venting would all need work at once. When every one of those boxes is ticked, the conversion can climb toward the top of the cost range, and a high-efficiency power-vent or condensing tank delivers most of the benefit for less. Our <a href="/best-gas-water-heater/">gas water heater picks</a> cover those options.
          </p>

          <p>
            Where conversion clearly makes sense is a larger household that runs out of hot water with the current tank, a home with an existing three-quarter-inch or larger gas line and an outside wall close to the heater, and owners planning to stay long enough to collect on a 20-year lifespan. If that is you, size it carefully, specify condensing to keep venting cheap, and choose from our <a href="/best-tankless-water-heater/">tested tankless water heater picks</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying the heater before the gas line is checked</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Ordering a 199,000 BTU unit online and then discovering the half-inch line and the meter cannot feed it is the most common conversion surprise. Have an installer total your connected gas load and measure the run first, then pick a heater the house can actually supply.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Reusing the old flue</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A gas tankless heater exhausts under positive pressure and is not approved for the B-vent or chimney the old tank used. Reusing it risks exhaust leaking into the house and fails inspection. The vent must be the type and length the manual specifies.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the furnace that shared the flue</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                When the water heater leaves a shared flue, the furnace can be left in a vent sized for two appliances. That orphaned furnace may draft poorly and condense inside the chimney. Have the installer check and price a liner or new vent before work starts.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping isolation valves</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Without service valves, every descaling means a plumber visit, so most owners skip it until scale damages the heat exchanger. The valves cost little at installation and make annual flushing a simple DIY job. Make them a line item on every quote.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Converting in an emergency</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Rushing a conversion the day the old tank fails leaves no time to upgrade a meter, compare quotes or size the unit properly. If you need hot water today, replace the tank like for like and plan the tankless conversion on your own schedule.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I replace my tank water heater with a tankless one?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, almost any home can, but it usually involves more than swapping the appliance. Gas units often need a larger gas line, a new direct vent, a condensate drain and a 120-volt outlet. Electric units need large dedicated circuits that many panels cannot supply without an upgrade. A site visit tells you which of those apply before you commit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much more does converting to tankless cost than replacing a tank?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Typically $1,500 to $3,500 more for gas, sometimes higher. A gas tankless replacing a tank commonly runs $3,000 to $6,500 installed, against $1,500 to $3,000 for a new gas tank. The difference is mostly gas line, venting and labor. Our tankless water heater cost guide breaks down each line item.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a bigger gas line for a tankless water heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually. A whole-house unit draws up to about 199,000 BTU per hour, four to five times a typical tank. A half-inch line can only carry that over a very short run, so most conversions add a three-quarter-inch line or larger. The installer sizes it from code tables using your total gas load and the distance from the meter.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a tankless water heater use my existing vent?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Generally not. Gas tankless heaters are fan-driven and vent under positive pressure, and manufacturers do not allow them on the B-vent or chimney an atmospheric tank used. Condensing models vent through PVC or polypropylene; non-condensing models need stainless steel. Either way, a new vent is run, usually out the nearest exterior wall.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long does a tank to tankless conversion take?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A simple conversion with a short gas run and an easy outside wall usually takes one full day. Long gas runs, roof venting, a condensate pump or an orphaned furnace flue can push it to two days. If the utility must upgrade the gas meter or the home needs an electrical service upgrade, scheduling can add weeks.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I install a tankless water heater myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                You can mount it and connect the water lines if you are handy, but gas piping, venting and electrical work often require a licensed professional, and a permit and inspection are almost always required. Most manufacturers also condition the heat exchanger warranty on licensed installation, so a DIY job can void the most valuable part of the purchase.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is replacing a water heater with tankless worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It is worth it for larger households that run out of hot water, homes with an adequate gas line and an easy vent route, and owners staying long enough to benefit from a 20-year lifespan. It is rarely worth it for small households on cheap gas, or all-electric homes with limited panel capacity, where a heat pump water heater is usually the better buy.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

