        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Replacing a water heater with tankless is worth doing when the house can supply it: enough gas line and meter capacity, a short path to an outside wall for the vent, and water that is soft or will be treated. When those line up, the conversion costs a modest premium over a new tank and gives you a heater that can outlast two of them.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            When they do not line up, the extra work stacks quickly. Get an itemized quote that prices the gas line, vent, condensate, power and valves separately, plan the job before your tank fails, and be willing to choose a heat pump or high-efficiency tank if the numbers point that way.
          </p>
        </div>

